# What a raised verbosity costs

**DONE.** Measured 2026-09-05. The client-side cost is nothing. The
DAEMON-side cost is real, and the probe found a defect beside it.
`tasks/096` is the fix.

## The question

`printMsg` gates on `nix::verbosity` in a MACRO, so the arguments are
lazy and a rejected message costs nothing (`logging.hh:327`). Raising
the global moves that: every site at or under the new level runs
`fmt(args)` and builds a string the tap may then drop.

`subscribe_logs(level=N)` raises it to N and never lowers, so a
process that once served a talkative subscriber formats at talkative
for the rest of its life.

## What was measured

`.scratchpad/095/`. Four conditions, one per subprocess, because the
global is monotonic and no process can hold two levels in turn. The
order rotates per repetition. 12 repetitions, medians reported.

The store is `auto`, which on this machine is the daemon
(`nix store info --store auto`: `Store URL: daemon`, `Version:
2.34.8`, `Trusted: 1`). The daemon is the same 2.34.8 the bindings
link, so no version skew.

Workload: 16 real store paths, 60 rounds each of `is_valid_path`,
`query_path_info` and `compute_fs_closure`.

| condition | `nix::verbosity` | subscriber | connection opened at |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `baseline` | 3 (lvlInfo) | none | 3 |
| `raised` | 7 (lvlVomit) | none - subscribed once, unsubscribed | 7 |
| `raised-quiet` | 7 | process-wide, kept open | 7 |
| `raised-after` | 7 | none | **3** |

`raised-after` is the attribution. The connection is LAZY, so the
raise happens after one warm-up call has already forced the handshake
at the default. Nothing raised reaches the wire, so its delta is
client-side `fmt` alone.

**lvlInfo is the baseline, not lvlError.** The task asked for
lvlError. It is not reachable: the global starts at lvlInfo and
`raise_verbosity` only goes up. lvlInfo is also the honest floor -
no real process runs below it.

## The numbers

Medians of 12, in milliseconds, for 960 calls (16 paths x 60 rounds):

| phase | baseline | raised | raised-quiet | raised-after |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `is_valid_path` | 40.9 | 58.0 | 59.7 | 38.6 |
| `query_path_info` | 3.1 | 3.3 | 3.9 | 3.0 |
| `compute_fs_closure` | 15.0 | 16.2 | 17.8 | 15.2 |

As a ratio to the baseline:

| phase | raised | raised-quiet | raised-after |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `is_valid_path` | 1.42x | 1.46x | **0.95x** |
| `query_path_info` | 1.05x | 1.24x | **0.95x** |
| `compute_fs_closure` | 1.08x | 1.19x | **1.01x** |

Three readings, and they agree:

1. **The client-side formatting cost is not measurable.**
   `raised-after` is the baseline within noise on all three phases.
   This confirms 089's count - libexpr holds 4 literal `printMsg(`
   and 29 across the macro family, against 228 tree-wide - and the
   probe that showed one record for a whole file evaluation.
2. **The whole cost is the daemon narrating over the socket.**
   `raised` and `raised-quiet` differ from the baseline by 1.05x to
   1.46x, and from each other by noise. `RemoteStore::setOptions`
   sends `nix::verbosity` (remote-store.cc:118) and `daemon.cc:239`
   assigns it, so the daemon's `TunnelLogger::log` (daemon.cc:88)
   starts formatting and enqueueing at vomit for every worker op.
3. **Writing to stderr is not the cost.** `raised-quiet` gives every
   record to a queue instead and is no faster.

`query_path_info` is nearly flat because `Store::queryPathInfo`
caches client-side: 960 calls produced 91 worker ops, not 960.

## What the probe found that it was not looking for

**A process that once subscribed prints daemon debug lines on stderr
forever, for every caller, subscribed or not.**

Deterministic. 1052 lines in every `raised` repetition, 0 in every
other condition:

```
    960 performing daemon worker op: 1
     91 performing daemon worker op: 26
```

960 = 16 paths x 60 rounds of `is_valid_path` (`wopIsValidPath`), 91
= the uncached `query_path_info` ops. The text is `daemon.cc:1091`,
a `debug()` inside the daemon.

The mechanism, read rather than guessed:

1. `raise_verbosity` moves `nix::verbosity` to 7 and never lowers it.
2. `RemoteStore::setOptions` sends that 7 to the daemon
   (remote-store.cc:118); `daemon.cc:239` assigns it to the daemon's
   own `nix::verbosity`.
3. The daemon's `TunnelLogger::log` now passes `lvl > verbosity` and
   sends the line back as `STDERR_NEXT` (daemon.cc:94).
4. The client reads it and calls
   **`printError(chomp(readString(from)))`**
   (`worker-protocol-connection.cc:75`).

Step 4 is the one that matters. It **ERASES the level**: a daemon
`debug()` arrives at the client as **lvlError**. lvlError is 0, so it
passes `LogTap::log`'s `effective_verbosity()` gate, passes the
fallback `SimpleLogger`'s `nix::verbosity` gate, and reaches
descriptor 2. No gate anywhere downstream can tell it from a real
error, because on the wire it no longer is one.

`unsubscribe_process_logs` does not help. It restores
`default_verbosity()` to lvlInfo, which is what `effective_verbosity`
reads - but the daemon was told 7 at handshake time and keeps it for
the life of the connection.

This refuted a comment in `logging.hpp` that said an unsubscribed
caller "sees exactly what it saw before the tap existed". Corrected
in its own commit.

## What follows

The task said: if the difference is large, the answer is probably to
LOWER the global when the last subscription that needed it goes.

The timing alone would not have carried that - 1.1x to 1.5x on store
ops, and only while someone asked. The stderr leak does, because it
is unbounded in time and reaches a caller who asked for nothing.

`tasks/096` holds the fix and its argument, including the part
lowering cannot reach: a connection already open keeps the level it
was given.

## What this is NOT

A reason to pin. The pin costs the daemon permanently and for every
process, which is the same defect with no subscription needed.
`tasks/089` records why it was removed, and this measurement is the
first direct evidence of what it would have cost.

Opened 2026-09-05, from `tasks/089` step 4. Measured the same day.
